Zero native preparation and UEFI file deployment

setup.zero is original Zero application code. It calls the original Zero
native host ABI directly on Linux or Windows. The delivered executable does
not launch a shell, Python, CMD, PowerShell or a foreign language runtime.
Build and test scripts are external development infrastructure only.

Commands:
  zero-setup prepare SOURCE.iso NEW_DIRECTORY
  zero-setup deploy SOURCE.iso LOADER.efi NEW_DIRECTORY
  zero-setup status DIRECTORY
  zero-setup remove DIRECTORY
Windows uses zero-setup.exe with the same arguments.

prepare preserves the version-1 preparation format: exact system.iso copy,
16 MiB original empty ZeroFS storage.data, and the ownership manifest
zero.install. This is preparation only, with no boot loader installed.

deploy adds the original Zero UEFI x64 loader as ZEROX64.EFI and writes a
version-2 manifest. Supply the matching loader from installer/boot/ or the
native toolkit. Both source files are checked before creating the destination.
The loader finds system.iso in its own directory through firmware Simple File
System and loads the existing kernel/tools directly from that ISO. No optical
medium or extracted root-level kernel.bin/tools.bin is needed for this path.

To launch the deployed files, put the complete directory on a filesystem that
UEFI firmware can read, normally FAT, and select ZEROX64.EFI using the firmware
file-launch menu. The loader is unsigned, so Secure Boot must not reject it.
Directory names may contain spaces and Unicode, within the loader's documented
UTF-16 path bound. The installer does not determine whether an arbitrary host
path is firmware-readable. It does not create firmware boot entries, register
Windows BCD or Linux GRUB entries, mount an EFI partition, modify partition
tables, overwrite another boot loader or change Secure Boot settings.

This is a usable UEFI file-launch path, not a complete Wubi installation.
Firmware normally cannot read a file inside arbitrary Windows NTFS or Linux
ext4/Btrfs volumes. BIOS and IA32 deployment are not provided by this command.
The original hybrid ISO still contains its existing BIOS/UEFI 32/64 boot paths.
See installer/boot/ for the loader implementation and actual no-CD VM evidence.

storage.data remains a prepared data file. The UEFI deployment loader does not
mount that host file as a kernel block device after ExitBootServices. A test
may explicitly attach it as a separate virtual ATA disk, but that is not
persistent host-file-backed Wubi storage. Actual host-file persistence and
automatic boot-menu integration remain unfinished.

Input validation and publication

The ISO must be the current 9,224-sector ZERO_SYSTEM BIOS/UEFI optical format.
The checker validates identity, paired-endian volume/block sizes and the
supported El Torito catalog. The loader must be a 1,024..65,536-byte PE32+ x64
EFI application. Checks include bounded DOS/PE/optional/section headers,
architecture, EFI subsystem, alignments, file and virtual section extents,
nonoverlapping ordered sections and an entry point in an executable section.
This validates the supported envelope; it does not prove arbitrary machine
code correct, safe, authored by Zero, or authenticated. Use the provided own
loader and a trusted ISO. Full file checksums detect accidental corruption.

Every destination must be a new exclusive directory. Paths must be below
4,000 bytes and cannot end with a separator, drive colon, '.' or '..' basename.
Each payload is written exclusively to a staging name, flushed through
filesync (Linux fsync / Windows FlushFileBuffers), closed, reopened and hashed,
then atomically published without replacing any existing path. The ownership
manifest is flushed, closed and byte-verified before being published last.
Any write/flush/close/readback failure prevents success. Failure cleanup only
removes paths exclusively created by that invocation and reports cleanup
failures. It never recursively removes unknown contents.

File flush is not a complete power-loss durability guarantee. There is no
parent-directory flush primitive, and a filesystem may lose directory entries
or rename publication after power failure. The manifest uses streaming FNV-1a64
implemented in Zero; it is not a signature or proof of publisher identity.
Neither the source ISO nor supplied loader is authenticated by this tool.
The host file ABI rejects final symlinks/reparse points, but ancestor links and
concurrent hostile directory replacement are not a sandboxed threat model.
Use a private directory and do not run simultaneous lifecycle operations.

Ownership manifest (256 bytes, u64 little-endian)

0 magic 0x315055544553525a (ZRSETUP1); 8 format version 1 or 2;
16 state 1; 24 ISO bytes 18890752; 32 ISO FNV-1a64;
40 data bytes 16777216; 48 initial data FNV-1a64;
56 preparation host 1 Linux / 2 Windows; 64 deployment flags;
72 data profile 1 ZeroFS; 240 FNV-1a64 of bytes 0..239; 248 reserved zero.
Version 1: flags 0 and bytes 80..239 zero.
Version 2: flags 1 means EFI file deployed, not a registered boot entry;
80 loader bytes; 88 loader FNV-1a64; bytes 96..239 zero.

Status and conservative removal

status verifies all immutable ISO/loader bytes and the manifest. Version 1
also requires the initial data checksum to match, preserving compatibility.
Version 2 accepts modified storage.data when its size is unchanged and at
least one ZeroFS snapshot is structurally valid. The checker matches kernel
magic/generation/header and record checksums, allowed unique names, record
lengths and text constraints. It reports modified data as preserved. One valid
snapshot is explicitly reported as degraded; two invalid snapshots fail.
This does not automatically attach the file to the running OS.

remove verifies every owned payload before its first deletion. It requires
storage.data to match the initial preparation checksum for both versions.
Any changed user data therefore blocks all removal, including ISO and loader
removal. No force/data-wipe option is provided. Unknown directory contents are
preserved; the directory is removed only if empty. A deletion I/O failure can
leave a partially removed set and is explicitly reported. Missing files and
access denial are not conflated with successful removal.

Exit codes: 0 success; 64 usage/path; 65 unsupported source/manifest;
73 exclusive create/publication; 74 read/write/flush/close I/O;
75 integrity verification; 76 removal I/O failure.

Verification

The 0.11 fixture suite currently passes 65 native Linux checks and 53 Windows
PE checks under Wine. It covers v1 compatibility, v2 deployment and independent
manifest checks, UTF-8 paths, malformed/truncated/oversized PE files, source
and deployed corruption, valid modified ZeroFS data, degraded snapshots,
refusal to remove used data, unknown-file preservation and partial-write
cleanup. Linux syscall injection forces EIO at all three prepare flushes and
all four deploy flushes; each fails closed and cleans up only owned files.
A manifest fsync EINTR is retried and verified before success.

Wine does not reproduce native Windows reparse behavior or Windows flush-error
injection. Actual Microsoft Windows execution remains unverified. Earlier
separate-disk ZeroFS persistence evidence is historical and is not host-file
persistence. Current machine-readable installer evidence is in
installer/verification/0.11/; loader boot evidence lives under installer/boot/.
